     "text": "```python\ndef longest_common_prefix(strs):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the longest common prefix string among a list of strings.\n    If the list is empty or all strings are empty, returns an empty string.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not strs:\n        return \"\"\n    \n    # Take the first string as the initial prefix\n    prefix = strs[0]\n    \n    # Iterate through each string in the list\n    for s in strs[1:]:\n        # Update the prefix to be the shorter of the current prefix and the string\n        prefix = prefix[:len(prefix)-1] if len(prefix) > len(s) else prefix[:len(s)]\n        \n        # If the prefix becomes empty, no common prefix exists\n        if not prefix:\n            return \"\"\n    \n    return prefix\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n- **Input Handling**: The function first checks if the input list is empty, returning an empty string in that case.\n- **Initial Prefix**: The first string in the list is used as the initial candidate for the common prefix.\n- **Iterative Comparison**: For each subsequent string, the prefix is shortened to the length of the shorter string, ensuring that the prefix remains a prefix of both the current string and the previously determined prefix.\n- **Early Termination**: If at any point the prefix becomes an empty string, the function returns early since no common prefix can exist.\n- **Return Value**: The function returns the longest common prefix found, or an empty string if none exists.",
