    {
     "text": "```python\ndef longest_common_prefix(strs):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the longest common prefix string among a list of strings.\n    If the list is empty or contains only empty strings, returns an empty string.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not strs:\n        return \"\"\n    \n    # Take the first string as the initial prefix\n    prefix = strs[0]\n    \n    # Iterate through each string in the list\n    for s in strs[1:]:\n        # Update the prefix by slicing it to the length of the shorter string\n        # and comparing character by character\n        prefix = prefix[:len(s)]\n        \n        # If the prefix becomes empty, no common prefix exists\n        if not prefix:\n            return \"\"\n        \n        # Update the prefix to the common part of the current string and the previous prefix\n        prefix = s[:len(prefix)]\n        \n        # If the prefix becomes empty, no common prefix exists\n        if not prefix:\n            return \"\"\n    \n    return prefix\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n- **Input Handling**: The function first checks if the input list is empty, returning an empty string in that case.\n- **Initial Prefix**: The first string in the list is used as the initial candidate for the common prefix.\n- **Iterative Comparison**: For each subsequent string, the prefix is shortened to the length of the current string, then compared character by character with the current string.\n- **Early Termination**: If at any point the prefix becomes empty, the function returns early since no common prefix can exist.\n- **Efficiency**: The algorithm runs in O(n*m) time complexity, where n is the number of strings and m is the length of the longest string, which is optimal for this problem.",
